[block'ORD-0024-modes'
  [ctx'the spec of EMS-002','.guidelines/.orders/EMS-002/EMS-002.pgml']
  [tgt'the mode words never vanish, by the inclusion the Regent decided on 2026-10-25: a stray on, an off as the last characters, and the bare words off and on; an ORD of the batch the version moves once for']
  [ctx'measured at b827782: a stray on, written with no off open before it, leaves the XML byte-identical to the source without it, the lexer marks it stray and nothing reads that; an off as the very last characters leaves no trace, because the lexer loop ends before the off branch can mark it unclosed, while an off followed by anything raises UnclosedOffMode; the bare words off and on are prose with no diagnostic, while the other 142 known names raise LooseCommandWord, because the known-word list leaves out MODE']
  [ctx'the rules that include them: the element naming of XML_REFERENCE section 2 gives on its element mode-on, and the inverse already writes mode-on back as a closed on, which the lexer then dropped; UnclosedOffMode for every unclosed off; the loose-word check for every known name; nada desaparece em silencio, .constraints']
  [cnst'outside an off region, a closed on is the command ON written closed, the same node an open on makes, with a note UnmatchedOnMode at its position naming that nothing was off; a chain operator before it is no longer a dangling chain']
  [cnst'an off at the end raises UnclosedOffMode like any other unclosed off, and an empty region emits an empty off element, read back as an off and an on; the empty command OFF reads back as modeoff, the element spelling read as input, never as the switch']
  [cnst'MODE joins the loose-word list, with the hint the others get; one declared source for the new code, OC-13, by M01, growing the snapshot with no hash moved; the Rust is ported byte for byte, held by new parse probes']
  [val'the modes','a stray on is the command with its note, an off at the end raises UnclosedOffMode, the bare words raise LooseCommandWord, and every case comes back to the same XML; each seen failing before it counts, and the Rust answers byte for byte']
];
